# half_exhaustive.expected: exhaustive f16 round-trip golden (S-009 §5.8,
# P1-18.2 — review 2026-11-21: "no exhaustive 65536-value f16 round-trip
# test (only 4 spot tests)").
# Provenance: C reference obsproject/obs-studio 33.0.1 (graphics/half.h
#   byte-identical to master), 64-bit Linux, -O2.
# Generator: test/conformance/gen/half_dump.c — for each of the 65536
#   bit patterns P: exact f16->f32 embedding (pure integer arithmetic,
#   the same algorithm in the Rust test), C half_from_float, one line
#   "half 0x<in> 0x<out>". blake2b-512 over the full dump.
# The Rust test (obs-math, half) rebuilds the same 65536 lines and
# compares digest + spots.
#
# Result recorded here: the round-trip is the identity for ALL 64512
# finite patterns (subnormals included — C half_from_float is the exact
# inverse on f16-representable f32 values; the R113 no-sticky quirk only
# affects f32 values between f16 subnormals, which are not f16
# patterns). Only NaN payloads collapse to the canonical 0x7fff (parity
# is the contract; the Rust port reproduces it).
